'''tritical†''' (trit′i-kal), ''a.'' [< ''trite'' + ''-ical'', appar. in imitation of ''critical''.] Trite; common.

{{smaller block|A tedious homily or a ''tritical'' declamation.
{{right|''I. D'Israeli'', Amen. of Lit., I. 326.}}}}

'''tritically†''' (trit′i-kal-i), ''adv.'' In a tritical or commonplace manner.

{{smaller block|This sermon upon the Jewish dispensation, . . . 'tis all tritical, and most ''tritically'' put together.
{{right|''Sterne'', Tristram Shandy, vi. 11.}}}}

'''triticalness†''' (trit′i-kal-nes), ''n.'' The state or character of being tritical; triteness.

'''triticeoglossus''' (tri-tis″ē-ō-glos′us), ''n.''; pl. ''triticeoglossi'' (-ī). [NL., < L. ''triticeus'', of wheat (see ''triticeous''), + Gr. γλῶσσα, the tongue.] A small muscle occasionally found in the human larynx, connected with the triticeous nodule in the posterior thyrohyoid membrane, and passing forward to the tongue.

'''triticeous''' (tri-tish′ius), ''a.'' [< L. ''triticeus'', of wheat, < ''triticum'', wheat: see ''Triticum''.] In ''anat.'', small and roundish, like a grain of wheat or millet-seed: nodular.—{{smaller|'''Triticeous nodule''', one of the small cartilaginous nodules in the larynx—the cartilago triticeus, or corpus triticeum.}}

'''triticeum''' (tri-tis′ē-um), ''n.''; pl. ''triticea'' (-ä̤). [NL., neut. (sc. ''corpus'', body) of L. ''triticeus'', of wheat: see ''triticeous''.] The triticeous body or nodule of the larynx: the triticeus.
